  "generated_model": "Mathematical Model for the Supplier-Supermarket Inventory Allocation Problem\n\nSets:\n- Let F = {S1, S2, ..., S12} be the set of suppliers.\n- Let C = {C1, C2, ..., C12} be the set of supermarkets (customers).\n\nParameters:\n- Fixed cost for opening supplier i ∈ F: \n  f = [98.88, 99.73, 94.01, 93.77, 107.59, 112.65, 97.05, 103, 90.45, 96.73, 96.43, 112.19]\n  where f_i is the fixed cost for supplier S_i, in the order S1–S12.\n\n- Demand for each supermarket j ∈ C:\n  d = [1097, 61, 11, 7, 82, 37, 483, 582, 223, 89, 60, 55]\n  where d_j is the demand for customer C_j, in the order C1–C12.\n\n- Transportation cost per unit from supplier i ∈ F to supermarket j ∈ C:\n  T = [t_ij] is a 12×12 matrix, where t_ij is the cost from supplier S_i to customer C_j:\n\n| Supplier \\ Customer |   C1   |   C2   |   C3   |   C4   |   C5   |   C6   |   C7   |   C8   |   C9   |   C10  |   C11  |   C12  |\n|---------------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n| S1                  | 284.11 | 53.78  | 10.62  | 111.27 | 158.5  | 8.79   | 53.79  | 8.84   | 1911.43| 8.87   | 1129.47| 185.53 |\n| S2                  | 7.19   | 1031.96| 90.94  | 276.97 | 0.45   | 0.2    | 49.14  | 1.05   | 2079.54| 1.45   | 49.14  | 0.05   |\n| S3                  | 151.1  | 884.48 | 4.33   | 277.04 | 0.33   | 0.19   | 49.14  | 0.99   | 99.03  | 1.63   | 884.47 | 0.96   |\n| S4                  | 144.16 | 868.75 | 94.2   | 285.48 | 16.93  | 0.94   | 868.78 | 16.6   | 98.69  | 19.74  | 868.74 | 19.85  |\n| S5                  | 151.34 | 1030.88| 91.43  | 13.24  | 0.72   | 0.87   | 49.09  | 0.01   | 99.05  | 0.84   | 883.6  | 0.58   |\n| S6                  | 7.18   | 49.13  | 90.72  | 277.57 | 0.37   | 0.58   | 1031.74| 0.76   | 1782.98| 1.06   | 884.31 | 0.34   |\n| S7                  | 104.38 | 1324.35| 1829.39| 1857.57| 1782.69| 2079.47| 1324.31| 2080.29| 0      | 2080.99| 1545.08| 99.07  |\n| S8                  | 129.51 | 1031.96| 4.33   | 276.97 | 0.02   | 0.23   | 884.56 | 1.22   | 2079.54| 1.69   | 49.14  | 0.05   |\n| S9                  | 50.93  | 5.75   | 1057.85| 58.62  | 47.63  | 1000.41| 103.48 | 47.6   | 1642.85| 47.59  | 5.75   | 999.94 |\n| S10                 | 129.62 | 884.35 | 91.10  | 277.12 | 0.27   | 0.07   | 1031.78| 0.91   | 99.03  | 0.08   | 49.13  | 0.04   |\n| S11                 | 53.3   | 0      | 941.91 | 58.92  | 1031.61| 49.13  | 0.03   | 1030.99| 1324.29| 49.1   | 0.08   | 49.12  |\n| S12                 | 959.55 | 0.11   | 941.98 | 1237.42| 49.13  | 1031.86| 0.09   | 1031.07| 73.57  | 49.1   | 0.12   | 1031.53|\n\nDecision Variables:\n- y_i ∈ {0,1} for each supplier i ∈ F: y_i = 1 if supplier i is open, 0 otherwise.\n- x_ij ≥ 0 for each supplier i ∈ F and supermarket j ∈ C: quantity of goods supplied from supplier i to supermarket j.\n\nMathematical Formulation:\n\nObjective:\nMinimize the total cost (fixed + transportation):\n\n\\[\n\\min \\sum_{i=1}^{12} f_i y_i + \\sum_{i=1}^{12} \\sum_{j=1}^{12} t_{ij} x_{ij}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( f_i \\) is the fixed cost for supplier S_i (see vector above),\n- \\( t_{ij} \\) is the transportation cost per unit from supplier S_i to customer C_j (see matrix above),\n- \\( x_{ij} \\) is the quantity supplied from S_i to C_j,\n- \\( y_i \\) is the binary variable indicating if supplier S_i is open.\n\nSubject to:\n\n1. Demand satisfaction for each supermarket:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_{ij} = d_j \\quad \\forall j = 1, ..., 12\n\\]\nwhere \\( d_j \\) is the demand for customer C_j.\n\n2. Supply only from open suppliers:\n\\[\nx_{ij} \\leq d_j y_i \\quad \\forall i = 1, ..., 12; \\quad \\forall j = 1, ..., 12\n\\]\n\n3. Variable domains:\n\\[\ny_i \\in \\{0,1\\} \\quad \\forall i = 1, ..., 12\n\\]\n\\[\nx_{ij} \\geq 0 \\quad \\forall i = 1, ..., 12; \\quad \\forall j = 1, ..., 12\n\\]\n\nAll parameters (fixed costs, demands, transportation costs) are explicitly given above. This model determines which suppliers to open and how to allocate supply to supermarkets to minimize total cost.",
